<div class="post-content">
  <p>
    Patternmaking is where product design meets reality. A pattern is a full-scale template for every piece that will be cut and sewn. Getting the pattern right means your finished product fits, seams align, and construction works cleanly—without surprise fit issues during assembly.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pattern-fundamentals">Pattern Fundamentals</h2>
  <p>
    A successful pattern accounts for several things working together:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Grain direction:</strong> The grain of your fabric matters for drape, stretch, and how seams will behave. Patterns note grain direction so pieces are cut consistently.</li>
    <li><strong>Seam allowance:</strong> The space between the stitching line and the edge of the cut piece. Standard seam allowances vary (1/4", 3/8", 1/2", 5/8") depending on the material and construction method.</li>
    <li><strong>Notches:</strong> Small cut marks that help you match pieces during assembly. Notches align grain, show grain direction, and prevent sewing pieces in the wrong order or rotation.</li>
    <li><strong>Balance marks:</strong> Lines and dots that show where seams should meet—essential for darts, princess seams, and anywhere precision alignment matters.</li>
    <li><strong>Piece labels:</strong> Clear labeling (Front, Back, Side, etc.) with grain arrows and cut quantities prevent mistakes at the cutting table.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="drafting-and-testing">Drafting and Testing</h2>
  <p>
    Before you cut production fabric, draft your pattern on kraft paper or muslin and test it. This is where you catch problems:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Do the pieces fit together as intended?</li>
    <li>Is the seam allowance adequate for your construction method?</li>
    <li>Will bulk at intersections prevent smooth seams?</li>
    <li>Does the shape match your design intent, or does it need adjustment?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Test construction—building a prototype with your draft pattern on muslin or similar material—is essential. This muslin sample (sometimes called a "toile") lets you refine fit, proportion, and construction before committing to production materials. Pattern adjustments are cheap on kraft paper; they're expensive after cutting expensive fabric.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pattern-scaling">Scaling and Grading</h2>
  <p>
    If you're making a bag in multiple sizes, you'll need a graded pattern set. Scaling isn't just uniform enlargement—proportion matters. A small clutch might have different strap lengths, gusset depths, or closure treatments than a large tote, even though the basic form is the same.
  </p>
  <p>
    Creating multiple sizes requires grading rules: how much do you add for each size? Common approaches include:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Even growth:</strong> Add the same amount to all dimensions (simple but doesn't work for all design features).</li>
    <li><strong>Proportional growth:</strong> Scale based on percentage of original size (better for maintaining proportions).</li>
    <li><strong>Selective growth:</strong> Add more to some areas (like length) and less to others (like strap width)—most design-conscious approach.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="pattern-tools">Pattern Tools and Materials</h2>
  <p>
    Good pattern drafting depends on the right tools:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Kraft paper or pattern paper:</strong> Inexpensive, erasable, and durable enough for testing and small production runs.</li>
    <li><strong>Rulers and straightedges:</strong> A clear ruler (to see through), a long ruler (24"+), and a T-square or set square for perpendicular lines.</li>
    <li><strong>Measuring tools:</strong> A flexible tape measure for body measurements, curves, and circumferences; a rigid ruler for straight edges.</li>
    <li><strong>Marking tools:</strong> A fine-tip pen (not pencil—it smudges) and an awl for punching notch marks.</li>
    <li><strong>Scissors:</strong> Sharp shears for cutting paper. Keep separate scissors for paper and fabric.</li>
    <li><strong>Curve templates:</strong> French curves or flexible curves for smooth, consistent curves (armholes, necklines, bag edges).</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Pattern quality compounds through every step of production. A pattern refined through test construction, clear labeling, and accurate piece layout is the foundation of a finished product you're proud of.
  </p>
</div>
